syntax:
To overload an operator, define a function with the keyword 'operator' followed by the operator symbol. For example:

// As a member function:
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass operator+(const MyClass& other) const;  // Binary operator as member
};

// As a non-member (free) function:
MyClass operator+(const MyClass& a, const MyClass& b);  // For symmetry

Practical usage notes: Use member functions for operators that naturally modify the object (e.g., =, ++), and non-member functions for binary operators to allow implicit conversions on the left operand. Always return by value or const reference where appropriate, and mark functions as const if they don't modify the object. In real-world code, this pattern enhances the idiomatic use of C++ containers and algorithms, like overloading << for easy debugging output.
